---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Closing from "../components/Closing.astro";
import CompareTable from "../components/CompareTable.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Feature from "../components/Feature.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Languages from "../components/Languages.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import QuickStart from "../components/QuickStart.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import CompetingOptions from "../components/demos/CompetingOptions.astro";
import DocsTiles from "../components/demos/DocsTiles.astro";
import DrillDown from "../components/demos/DrillDown.astro";
import EvidenceTabs from "../components/demos/EvidenceTabs.astro";
import Footprint from "../components/demos/Footprint.astro";
import Grammars from "../components/demos/Grammars.astro";
import HistoryTimeline from "../components/demos/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import Measures from "../components/demos/Measures.astro";
import OwnModel from "../components/demos/OwnModel.astro";
import Pipeline from "../components/demos/Pipeline.astro";
import ProposalSwitch from "../components/demos/ProposalSwitch.astro";
import ClaimMarks from "../components/demos/ClaimMarks.astro";
import TalkRelay from "../components/demos/TalkRelay.astro";
import WalkthroughPlayer from "../components/demos/WalkthroughPlayer.astro";
import { links } from "../lib/links";

// The site's name for search results: https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/site-names
const website = {
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "WebSite",
	name: "Archboard",
	url: new URL(import.meta.env.BASE_URL, Astro.site).href,
};
---

<Base
	title="Archboard: understand the code your agents write"
	description="Architecture modeling for codebases built with coding agents. Plan structural changes in Git, trace flows to source symbols, and verify the model against real code."
>
	<script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(website)} />
	<Nav />
	<main id="main">
		<Hero />
		<Languages />

		<Section id="understand" title="Follow a feature into the code">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Start from the root architecture and drill straight into individual services. Follow a
					single request across modules, then check what the model claims against the code.
				</p>
			</Fragment>

			<Feature title="Drill from the system into a service" flip>
				<p>
					Open any module to inspect its internal parts and the calls crossing its boundary, then
					open a part to go deeper. Breadcrumbs move back up the same tree, with every view derived
					from a single set of module definitions.
				</p>
				<p>Select the Inventory service card to open it.</p>
				<DrillDown slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<Feature title="Trace one request, step by step" wide>
				<p>
					Model a checkout flow with every call, return, failure branch, and retry loop, each
					anchored to the source symbol that sends it.
				</p>
				<p>
					Architecture, sequence, and flowchart views all render from the same model and share
					module identities. When a proposal alters a flow, the diff shows which messages changed.
				</p>
				<Grammars slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<EvidenceTabs />

			<Feature title="Spot shallow modules and coupling" wide>
				<p>
					Measures surfaces structural counts alongside Git history. Sort any column to find modules
					with wide interfaces, pass-through entry points, or frequent co-changes.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>High entry-point counts with few hidden parts flag potentially shallow modules.</li>
					<li>
						Unjoined pairs highlight modules that change in the same commits despite having no
						modeled relationship.
					</li>
					<li>
						No composite score. The diagnostics follow John Ousterhout's <em>A Philosophy of
						Software Design</em>, leaving the engineering call to you.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<Measures slot="demo" />
			</Feature>
		</Section>

		<Section id="talk" title="Talk to your architecture">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Hit Talk and ask questions out loud while browsing the canvas. The real-time Codex voice
					can inspect source files, draft architecture proposals, and run implementation work in
					your checkout.
				</p>
				<p>
					A low-latency coordinator answers architectural questions directly and delegates heavy
					work to a full Codex thread in your repo. Command approvals surface right in the pane, so
					you can approve or reject them by voice.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<div class="talk-stage" data-reveal>
				<TalkRelay />
				<p class="caption">
					Sample session on the example web shop. Live sessions use audio; this preview replays the
					transcript silently.
				</p>
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section id="plan" title="Diff the architecture before coding">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Have your agent draft changes as a proposal against the live architecture. Additions show
					in green, edits in orange, and removals in dashed red.
				</p>
				<p>The current model stays untouched until the work ships and you adopt the proposal.</p>
			</Fragment>

			<div class="plan-stage" data-reveal>
				<ProposalSwitch />
				<p class="caption">
					The example web shop's Queued fulfilment proposal: checkout returns as soon as payment
					starts, while a background worker reserves inventory and sends the receipt. Diff counts
					come from <code>archboard model compare</code>.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="sub-head" data-reveal>
				<h3 class="display feature-title">Compare options side by side</h3>
				<p class="body">
					Branch two proposals from the same baseline and inspect them in split panes. Both diff
					against the current architecture without modifying it.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="options-wrap">
				<CompetingOptions />
			</div>

			<Feature title="Verify the branch against the plan">
				<p>
					Once implementation starts, the branch footprint maps your Git diff through each module's
					code binding and compares it against the proposal.
				</p>
				<p>
					Scope creep outside the proposal and planned modules left untouched both surface before
					code review.
				</p>
				<Footprint slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<Feature title="Keep decisions and exact history" flip>
				<p>
					Once a change ships, adopt the proposal with the reasoning behind it. The previous state
					is preserved as an exact checkpoint, and shelved proposals keep their rationale too.
				</p>
				<p>
					Historical checkpoints live in a separate Git archive that you publish explicitly, while
					active modules, flows, proposals, and walkthroughs stay in the repo alongside source PRs.
				</p>
				<HistoryTimeline slot="demo" />
			</Feature>
		</Section>

		<Section id="how" title="Zero manual layout">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Your agent authors structured semantics: modules, containment, relationships, and request
					flows. The renderer computes every position and route from that data, so no vault file
					ever stores coordinates or layout overrides.
				</p>
				<p>
					Architecture updates happen in the same agent task as the code change, and the code check
					flags broken bindings or missing relationships. When the renderer improves, every view and
					historical checkpoint redraws cleanly.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<Pipeline />
			<p class="caption engine" data-reveal>
				A single Rust layout and painting core compiles to WebAssembly for both the CLI and the
				browser, using bundled fonts so interactive views and exported SVG or PNG files render
				identically.
			</p>

			<Feature title="Agents work in plain sight">
				<p>
					Before editing, an agent claims the modules or states it intends to change. Active claims
					show up directly in the sidebar and detail panel with a live status indicator and the
					agent's current task.
				</p>
				<p>
					Claims are purely informational and require no action from you. Every write includes the
					exact model revision it read, so the server rejects stale edits automatically.
				</p>
				<ClaimMarks slot="demo" />
			</Feature>

			<div class="sub-head" data-reveal>
				<h3 class="display feature-title">Archboard draws itself</h3>
				<p class="body">
					Archboard models its own codebase inside its repository and evolves it through proposals
					using the same CLI commands.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div data-reveal>
				<OwnModel />
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section id="share" title="Share diagrams and walkthroughs">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Embed tracked SVGs and PNGs in design docs, ADRs, and pull requests, then regenerate
					outdated renders from their recipes in one command. For complex changes, record a
					step-by-step walkthrough.
				</p>
			</Fragment>

			<DocsTiles />

			<div class="sub-head" data-reveal>
				<h3 class="display feature-title">Walk reviewers through the change</h3>
				<p class="body">
					A walkthrough sequences views across the model to explain a subsystem or proposal. Hit
					Narrate to have the Codex voice talk through each beat as the canvas highlights the
					relevant modules, or render the whole tour to MP4 from the CLI for a pull request.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="walk" data-reveal>
				<WalkthroughPlayer />
				<div class="walk-foot">
					<p class="caption">
						The example web shop's walkthrough, “How an order is placed today”. Live playback
						narrates each beat aloud via Codex; this preview runs muted.
						<a href={links.walkthroughs}>Watch a narrated pull-request video</a>.
					</p>
					<pre class="walk-cmd mono" tabindex="0"><span class="ps">$</span> archboard model record --checkpoint &lt;id&gt; --walkthrough "Why checkout stops waiting" --out pr-128.mp4</pre>
				</div>
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section id="compare" title="Where Archboard fits">
			<Fragment slot="lead">
				<p>
					Code visualizers inspect code after the fact, and diagram tools drift away from the repo.
					Archboard keeps one versioned model in Git so planning, review, and code checks all
					operate on the same definitions.
				</p>
			</Fragment>
			<CompareTable />
		</Section>

		<Section id="quick-start" title="Get started">
			<QuickStart />
		</Section>

		<Section id="faq" title="Questions">
			<Faq />
		</Section>

		<Closing />
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Base>

<style>
	.plan-stage,
	.talk-stage {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 16px;
	}
	.plan-stage .caption,
	.talk-stage .caption,
	.walk-foot .caption {
		max-width: 52em;
	}
	.caption code {
		font-size: 0.85em;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	/* A heading inside a section, for a demonstration that is not a feature row. */
	.sub-head {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
		align-items: end;
		gap: 16px 96px;
		margin: clamp(88px, 10vw, 140px) 0 32px;
	}
	/* The renderer's technical detail sits under the pipeline, below the outcome it explains. */
	.engine {
		max-width: 52em;
		margin: 20px 0 clamp(88px, 10vw, 152px);
	}
	.options-wrap {
		margin-bottom: clamp(88px, 10vw, 152px);
	}
	.walk {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 16px;
	}
	.walk-foot {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: baseline;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 12px 32px;
	}
	.walk-cmd {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--ink-muted);
		overflow-x: auto;
		max-width: 100%;
	}
	.ps {
		color: var(--gold-mark);
	}
	@media (max-width: 900px) {
		.sub-head {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
</style>
